Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> Doug Freyburger wrote:
>
> Two or more pets are always a useful company (if you have the whistle)
> throughout the whole game; you can use them in the corridors and in
> the maze to keep monsters at distance by blocking them when they try
> to approach. Interesting even if the monster vs. pet level is large
> enough that the pets don't attack.

Hostiles move directly toward you ignoring your pet.  Hostiles will fire
at you even if it is through other hostiles, peacefuls or pets.  I see a
lot of orcs kill each other in the rush to get to me.  But I can't tell
the difference between a monster attacking my pet to get past it to me,
and my pet attacking a monster first.  In a corridor it tends to happen
a couple of squares out of my sight.

Do pets push powerful hostiles back while advancing in corridors?  A lot
of times I displace my pet and start combat with a hostile. The kill
enough seems easy so I figure my pet did plenty of damage. I rarely
know if the hostile or my pet started fighting - When the hostile is
so powerful the pet would not have attacked it that it.

>> Very late in the game I might be able to tame pets in droves.  I can
>> wade through a crowd of pets or fight my way through a crowd of
>> hostiles.  If the crowd of hostiles is balrogs and archons it's not an
>> easy fight.  I'd rather Death fight with one of a dozen pets to get to
>> me than fight with me.
>
> A lot of pets at Astral may be a hindrance if you intended to use conflict
> or your wand of death against two of the riders.

They do seem to be mutually exclusive tactics on Astral.


